# ============================================================================
# This project is developed on Windows, where git's autocrlf turns LF into CRLF
# in the working tree. For most files that is harmless. For a shell script it
# is not: `#!/bin/sh` followed by a carriage return makes the kernel look for
# an interpreter named "sh\r", and the error names a file that plainly exists.
#
# The build and packaging scripts are also handed to a Linux container, so they
# have to be LF wherever they are checked out.
# ============================================================================
* text=auto

*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
